/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/reference/sandbox-api.md-232-- An adapter should ensure in-flight operations settle when the sandbox dies, by whatever mechanism its provider SDK supports — native rejection of in-flight calls, or polling a cheap control-plane status read while a call is pending. The first-party Cloudflare adapter implements the polling shape internally.
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/reference/sandbox-api.md-233-- An adapter with no such mechanism carries an accepted limitation: when the provider transport never settles a call after the sandbox dies, that call may hang until the surrounding operation is aborted.
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/reference/sandbox-api.md:234:- There is deliberately no per-command deadline in this contract. Agent commands are legitimately unbounded; `timeoutMs` is the command's own deadline, not an infrastructure liveness bound.
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/reference/sandbox-api.md-235-- An adapter that detects sandbox death should reject with `SandboxDiedError` (`type: 'sandbox_died'`, exported from `@flue/runtime`), so shell classification reports an infrastructure failure rather than caller cancellation.
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/reference/sandbox-api.md-236-- Liveness detection and caller abort are separate concerns implemented at different layers, and a death detector must not blur them: it races the sandbox's liveness signal against the in-flight provider call and nothing else. `sandboxFromDriver` already races `signal` one layer up and consumes the provider promise's eventual settlement once the caller has been released, so a death detector that also listens for `signal` produces two rejections competing for the same promise. The first-party Cloudflare adapter's death detector follows this split — liveness-only, with the caller-abort race left entirely to the wrapper it builds on.
