/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/reference/sandbox-api.md-370-
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/reference/sandbox-api.md-371-- Commands run through real `bash` when present (probed once per process, resolved to an absolute path), falling back to the platform default shell (`/bin/sh` or, on Windows, the system shell) when it is not.
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/reference/sandbox-api.md:372:- On POSIX the child leads its own process group; abort and timeout signal the whole group — `SIGTERM`, escalating to `SIGKILL` after a 2-second grace — so compound commands cannot orphan grandchildren. The kill is real, so `local()` has no orphaned-command window beyond that grace period.
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/reference/sandbox-api.md:373:- Non-zero exits and spawn failures resolve as `ShellResult` (spawn failures as `exitCode: 1` with the error message on stderr). A `timeoutMs` expiry also resolves as a `ShellResult`, with `exitCode: 124` — the `timeout(1)` convention. A caller-initiated `signal` abort instead rejects with `AbortError`, even though the kill takes the same process-group path and produces that same 124 exit internally — the rejection wins and the caller never observes the `ShellResult`. A signal death `local()` did not itself initiate keeps the generic `exitCode: 1`.
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/reference/sandbox-api.md-374-- Captured output is capped at 64 MiB; exceeding it kills the process tree and resolves with `exitCode: 1` and a truncation note on stderr.
