/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/reference/agent-api.md-182-- Calling `dispatch()` before a runtime is configured rejects — inside a Flue-built server the runtime is configured automatically; standalone scripts call [`start()`](#start) first.
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/reference/agent-api.md-183-- A missing `id` rejects with a human-readable `Error`; a malformed `message` throws `InvalidRequestError`.
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/reference/agent-api.md:184:- A dispatch to a busy instance joins the live response at the next turn boundary; a dispatch to an idle instance wakes a new response. Deliveries that miss the live response run as their own submission from the durable queue — they are never lost. Dispatched activity belongs to the continuing instance and shares one accepted order with direct HTTP prompts to it.
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/reference/agent-api.md-185-- **Target differences.** On Cloudflare, dispatch durably admits work to the target agent's Durable Object and may retry processing after an interruption. On Node, delivery durability follows the configured [persistence adapter](/docs/reference/data-persistence-api/): the default in-memory store is process-lifetime only, while a durable adapter keeps admitted dispatches across restarts and reconciles them on the replacement process. On both targets processing is at-least-once — design external side effects to be idempotent.
