/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/schedules.md-187-### Missed fires
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/schedules.md-188-
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/schedules.md:189:An in-process Node scheduler fires only while the server is running: fires during downtime or a deploy are skipped, and cron libraries do not replay them on restart. If a fire must not be lost to a restart window, use a platform scheduler (a Cloudflare Cron Trigger or an [external scheduler](#external-schedulers)) — the platform fires regardless of your process — or track the last completed run yourself and catch up at startup.
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/schedules.md-190-
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/schedules.md:191:On Cloudflare, the platform invokes the `scheduled` handler on cadence with no traffic required. Nothing durable exists until `dispatch(...)` resolves, so a handler that throws before admission delivers nothing for that fire; keep the handler thin — dispatch and return.
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/schedules.md-192-
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/schedules.md-193-### Overlap
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/schedules.md-194-
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/schedules.md:195:Deliveries to one conversation never run concurrently: inputs are processed in accepted order, and a message that arrives while a response is in flight joins it at a turn boundary. Overlapping fires against a fixed id therefore queue or coalesce — they cannot double-run the agent. Croner's `protect: true` additionally skips a fire while the previous callback is still executing, which matters when the callback awaits a settled reply rather than a fast admission. Per-fire ids are independent conversations and do run concurrently.
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/schedules.md-196-
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/schedules.md-197-### Durability
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/schedules.md-198-
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/schedules.md:199:`dispatch(...)` resolves at admission, and what admission guarantees depends on the target. On Node with the in-memory default, admitted work lasts only as long as the process — configure a durable [database](/docs/guide/database/) so accepted submissions survive a restart and a replacement process recovers them. On Cloudflare, admission is durable in the agent's Durable Object and interrupted processing is reconciled, which makes delivery **at-least-once** — design a scheduled agent's external side effects to be idempotent. [Durability](/docs/guide/durability/) covers what recovery replays on each target.
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/schedules.md-200-
