/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/cloudflare-target.md-108-The submitting connection observes the work but does not own it. If a client disconnects after admission, backend work can continue. Agent events are durably stored and can be replayed from any offset via the Durable Streams protocol.
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/cloudflare-target.md-109-
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/cloudflare-target.md:110:Accepted work runs inside the Durable Object itself, as one platform-visible unit per response: after admission answers, Flue schedules a zero-delay wake, and the object's alarm invocation claims the queued submission and awaits the full response — every model turn and tool call — before completing. Admission always returns first; the response begins on the next alarm tick, typically within tens of milliseconds. Three consequences:
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/cloudflare-target.md-111-
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/cloudflare-target.md-112-- **Platform observability sees agent work.** Workers Logs, Workers Traces, and invocation-wrapping integrations (the Sentry `wrap` pattern in [Extending Agents](#extending-agents-on-cloudflare)) attribute the whole response to the one invocation that ran it. [`createCloudflareTracing()`](#createcloudflaretracing) (installed by default) adds agent-level spans to those traces. See [Observability](/docs/guide/observability/#cloudflare) for the two views and [Deploy on Cloudflare](/docs/ecosystem/deploy/cloudflare/#observability) for enabling them.
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/cloudflare-target.md:113:- **Scheduled callbacks wait for a running response.** A Durable Object runs one alarm at a time, so `schedule()`/`scheduleEvery()` callbacks that come due mid-response fire after it settles — delivery is durable; timeliness is not guaranteed while the agent is busy. `queue()` is not alarm-driven and is unaffected. Steering is also unaffected: a message that arrives mid-response still joins it at the next turn boundary.
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/cloudflare-target.md-114-- **CPU limits are per invocation.** Agent responses are I/O-bound and sit far below the default 30-second active-CPU limit; raise [`limits.cpu_ms`](https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/platform/limits/) in `wrangler.jsonc` if an agent computes heavily.
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/cloudflare-target.md-115-
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/cloudflare-target.md:116:When a Durable Object resumes after interruption, Flue decides what to do next from the stored input and canonical conversation progress. It requeues only when it can prove the input was not applied, recognizes already-completed output, and records an interruption instead of blindly repeating uncertain model or tool work.
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/cloudflare-target.md-117-
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/cloudflare-target.md-118-For the full recovery model, see [Durability](/docs/guide/durability/).
--
