/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md-9-## Submissions and the accepted-work contract
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md-10-
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md:11:Every input that reaches an agent — a direct HTTP prompt, a [`dispatch(...)`](/docs/guide/building-agents/#dispatch) call, an [`init()` handle](/docs/reference/agent-api/#init)'s `dispatch(...)`, a [channel](/docs/guide/channels/) delivery, a [scheduled](/docs/guide/schedules/) trigger — is admitted as a **submission**: the payload is recorded durably _before_ any model work begins. That admission record is what the `202` response and the dispatch receipt attest to, and it carries an obligation:
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md-12-
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md-13-> Every accepted submission reaches exactly one durable terminal outcome — `completed`, `failed`, or `aborted` — no matter how many crashes happen in between.
--
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md-15-The outcome is written as a `submission_settled` record in the conversation's canonical stream, so anything waiting on the work observes it even across its own reconnects: the Flue Agent SDK's [`wait()`](/docs/sdk/flue-client/#wait) resolves or rejects from that record, and an awaited `init().read(...)` resolves with the settled reply or rejects with the settled error.
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md-16-
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md:17:Submissions for one conversation form a durable queue processed in admission order: one submission runs at a time, messages that arrive while the agent is busy either join the live response at a turn boundary or wait their turn, and a queued message is never lost — a delivery that misses the live response runs as its own submission. Processing happens in **attempts**: a coordinator claims the submission, runs it, and settles it. An interruption consumes the attempt; recovery claims a new one, up to the [retry budget](#retry-budget-and-timeout).
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md-18-
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md-19-Aborts follow the same discipline. `POST /:id/abort` (or the SDK's `abort()`) records a durable abort intent on every unsettled submission for the conversation; each one then settles with the distinct `aborted` outcome through the normal attempt machinery — even when the process that was running the work is already gone. Work that already completed is unaffected: an abort that arrives after a finished response settles `completed`.
--
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md-27-| Durable evidence after the input                | What recovery does                                                                  |
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md-28-| ----------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md:29:| The input was never persisted                   | Requeues the submission for a clean first attempt.                                  |
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md:30:| A completed assistant response                  | Settles `completed` — finished work is never discarded, even past the retry budget. |
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md-31-| A partial response with text or reasoning       | Tells the model its stream was interrupted and continues from the durable partial.  |
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md-32-| A tool turn with unresolved calls               | Repairs the tool batch (below), then continues the turn loop.                       |
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md:33:| A transient provider error (rate limit, outage) | Retries the turn after a backoff, under a bounded error budget.                     |
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md-34-| A context-overflow response                     | Compacts the conversation and retries the turn.                                     |
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md-35-| A durable abort intent                          | Settles `aborted`.                                                                  |
--
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md-39-The overall discipline is **at-least-once execution over exactly-once recording**. Work that committed durably — recorded responses, recorded tool results, committed state writes — never re-runs. Work that was interrupted before committing re-runs on the next attempt, which includes your [event hook](/docs/guide/agent-hooks/#event-hooks) callbacks: their durable effects commit atomically and never duplicate, but an external side effect inside one (an email, a page) may rarely happen twice. Guard anything that must not repeat with [persistent state](#persisted-state) or application-level idempotency.
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md-40-
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md:41:A recovered conversation always comes to rest in a state where the next message processes normally — an interrupted submission cannot wedge the queue behind it. The interruption stays visible in the timeline: the aborted partial, any interrupted-tool markers, and (on a failed settlement) a terminal advisory signal.
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md-42-
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md:43:## Retry budget and timeout
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md-44-
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md:45:Each interruption consumes one attempt. When a submission exhausts its attempts, or exceeds its wall-clock timeout, retrying stops: the conversation is settled to a rest state, a `submission_interrupted` advisory lands in the timeline, and the submission settles `failed` — waiters reject with the structured error, including which tool calls were left with unknown outcomes.
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md-46-
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md:47:The timeout is enforced preemptively, not just between attempts. The coordinator supervises running attempts on its wake cadence: at the deadline it fires the attempt's abort signal, so work suspended on a signal-aware await (a provider call, a sandbox command, any tool — a `run` that ignores its signal is abandoned rather than awaited) unwinds and settles through the normal paths — and an attempt hung below the abandonable layer is settled `failed` over the hung fiber after a short grace, its late writes fenced off. A hang delays settlement by at most the deadline plus that grace; it can never strand the submission. Most stalls never reach the deadline at all: a model stream that goes silent past its [idle timeout](/docs/reference/provider-api/#cloudflarebindingprovider) fails as a transient provider error and the turn retries under the [error budget](#recovery-after-an-interruption).
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md-48-
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md-49-The defaults are 10 attempts and one hour per submission. Override them per agent with the `durability` static:
--
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md-58-}
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md-59-
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md:60:IssueTriage.durability = { maxAttempts: 5, timeoutMs: 7_200_000 };
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md-61-```
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md-62-
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md:63:The static is applied by the platform while the agent function is _not_ running, so it stays in force after a crash — including a crash in the agent's own render. The timeout is the total wall-clock budget from the first attempt's start; turn-boundary joins and response continuations do not extend it. Full field reference: [`DurabilityConfig`](/docs/reference/agent-api/#durabilityconfig).
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md-64-
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md-65-## Durable tools and `step.do`
--
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md-98-## Delegated tasks
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md-99-
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md:100:A [subagent](/docs/guide/subagents/) task runs as a child session with its own durable conversation stream, so a crash mid-task loses none of the child's progress. When recovery repairs a tool batch containing an unresolved `task` call, it does not settle the call with a marker — it reattaches to the child's durable transcript, resumes the child to completion under the same recovery rules described above, and commits the child's real final result as the parent's tool outcome. This recurses: a child interrupted inside _its_ delegate resumes the grandchild first. Several tasks interrupted in one parallel batch are all resumed before the batch commits.
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md-101-
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md:102:A delegate has no durability configuration of its own: resumed child work runs inside the parent's attempt, under the parent's retry budget and timeout.
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md-103-
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md-104-Two edge cases:
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md-105-
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md-106-- **A delegate removed by a redeploy.** If the subagent is no longer declared when recovery runs, that one call settles with an error outcome and the parent continues; a renamed or removed delegate cannot be resumed under any retry.
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md:107:- **Terminal settlement.** When a submission exhausts its budget with a task still unresolved, the interrupted marker written for that call carries the child's conversation id, so the child's durable transcript remains inspectable.
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md-108-
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md-109-## Persisted state
--
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md-121-On Node, a coordinator inside your server process owns submission processing. Ownership is lease-based: each running submission carries a short lease that the owning process heartbeats while working. Recovery has two triggers:
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md-122-
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md:123:- **Startup reconciliation.** A replacement process scans for interrupted work when it boots and requeues it, then begins serving immediately while that work settles in the background. Ordering is preserved per conversation — recovered work runs ahead of newly delivered work, so a restart never reorders a conversation's timeline.
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md-124-- **Periodic lease scans.** While running, the coordinator scans for expired leases, so work stranded by a fast restart — where the new process boots before the old lease expires — is reclaimed within seconds rather than waiting for another restart.
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md-125-
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md:126:Graceful shutdown aborts active submissions at the turn boundary and waits for them to settle; work that does not settle in time is left running with its lease intact, and the next startup reclaims it after expiry.
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md-127-
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md-128-Two consequences for deployment:
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md-129-
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md-130-- **Recovery is only as durable as the database.** With the in-memory default, accepted work survives interruptions within the process lifetime but a restart loses everything; cross-restart recovery requires a durable adapter in [`db.ts`](/docs/guide/database/).
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md:131:- **One live owner per conversation.** A shared database lets a _replacement_ process recover accepted work, but it does not make two concurrent owners of the same conversation safe. Multi-replica deployments must route each conversation to one owner and avoid overlapping owners during replacement.
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md-132-
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md-133-See the [Node.js target guide](/docs/guide/node-target/#state-and-durability) for the rest of the target's behavior.
--
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md-138-
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md-139-- **Wake on start.** Whenever the Durable Object starts — after an eviction, a code deploy, or a platform reset — Flue immediately flags any attempt that was running when the previous instance died and reconciles it before serving new work. The platform's fiber-recovery callback triggers the same reconciliation path.
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md:140:- **A durable wake schedule.** While unsettled work exists, the object keeps a short self-renewing wake scheduled, so an interrupted submission recovers promptly even if no external request ever arrives to wake the object. Each wake runs a bounded supervision pass — reconcile, enforce deadlines, start work — and re-arms its successor before doing anything that can fail, so a hung attempt or a failed pass can delay supervision by at most one wake, never break it. Attempt execution runs detached from the wake that started it.
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md-141-
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md-142-Abort intents, attempt bookkeeping, and settlement records all live in the object's own storage, so an abort requested while the object was evicted is honored on the next wake. See the [Cloudflare target guide](/docs/guide/cloudflare-target/#durable-agent-execution) for the target's execution model.
--
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md-156-### Code outside the agent
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md-157-
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md:158:Flue does not checkpoint arbitrary TypeScript execution and resume a function from its last completed line. The checkpoint boundary is the agent itself: _inside_ it, a [durable tool](#durable-tools-and-stepdo) gives application-controlled work resumable `step.do` checkpoints backed by the conversation's own durability. _Outside_ it — the endpoint, script, or cron job that drives the agent — use the workflow engine your platform provides (Cloudflare Workflows, Inngest, or plain re-runs) and treat Flue like any other service you call from it. Redelivering a message is a new submission in the conversation, and the durable record shows what the previous attempt completed. The [Workflows](/docs/guide/workflows/) guide shows these patterns.
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md-159-
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/durability.md-160-### External side effects
--
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/node-target.md-7-The Node.js target builds your agents as a standard Node.js server. The built server runs anywhere Node runs: a local machine, a container, a VM, a CI runner, or a managed hosting service. Node is also the target where agents can operate directly on the host filesystem and shell through `local()`.
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/node-target.md-8-
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/node-target.md:9:For a deployment walkthrough, see [Deploy Agents on Node.js](/docs/ecosystem/deploy/node/). To run agents on a cron schedule, see [Schedules](/docs/guide/schedules/).
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/node-target.md-10-
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/node-target.md-11-## The built server
--
