/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/migration.md-200-   ```
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/migration.md-201-
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/migration.md:202:   A `signal` on the beta workflow call aborted the run itself; on the handle, `read(receipt, { signal })` cancels only the local wait — the submission keeps running and spending. Carrying the option over mechanically converts a durable abort into a local cancel. When cancelling the read should also stop the run, call [`abort()`](/docs/reference/agent-api/#agenthandle).
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/migration.md-203-
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/migration.md-204-2. **Checkpointed side-effect sequences inside an agent**: a `durable: true` tool with `step.do(...)`.
--
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/migration.md-206-3. **Multi-step orchestration with its own durability, retries, and inspection** (what workflow runs gave you): an application-owned orchestrator. On Cloudflare, use a [Cloudflare Workflow](https://developers.cloudflare.com/workflows/) whose steps call the `init()` handle: one step calls `dispatch(...)`, with the recorded receipt standing in for that step's result, and a following step calls `read(receipt)`, with the recorded reply standing in for its own result on re-execution. Run inspection (`getRun()`) has no framework replacement: reconcile from your orchestrator's own state and from `submission_settled` observability events.
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/migration.md-207-
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/migration.md:208:The retry policy that lived on the workflow moves to the agent's `durability` static. Scheduled workflows follow the same move: a cron trigger now dispatches a signal message to an agent (`dispatch(Agent, { id, message: { kind: 'signal', ... } })`) instead of calling `invoke()` — see [Schedules](/docs/guide/schedules/).
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/migration.md-209-
/home/ubuntu/.npm/_npx/8cba94e94504a6dc/node_modules/@flue/runtime/docs/guide/migration.md:210:In standalone Node scripts (cron jobs, CI, tests), boot the runtime first with `start()` from `@flue/runtime/node`, passing agent functions (or `{ agent, name? }` entries); then `init()`/`dispatch()` work as they do in a server. See [Standalone scripts](/docs/guide/building-agents/#standalone-scripts). All of these replacement patterns are covered in depth in the [Workflows](/docs/guide/workflows/) guide.
